# A method called on an element read straight out of a list -- `(l.get i: 0)`
# as the receiver -- binds that element to a temp first, and the temp is a
# BORROW: the element still belongs to the list. The temp was given the
# element's destructor, so a list of lists freed its element's buffer at the
# end of the statement and the next read used freed memory.
main: function is {
    lists: List (ListVal i64)
    first: ListVal i64
    first.append from: 1
    first.append from: 2
    lists.append from: first.take
    x: (lists.get i: 0).get i: 1
    y: (lists.get i: 0).contains item: 1
    z: (lists.get i: 0).contains item: 7
    print "element \{x}, has 1: \{y}, has 7: \{z}"
    for l: lists.iterate loop {
        print "the list still holds \{l.length}"
    }
}
